  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 AM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nToday-Wednesday:\n\nUpper level shortwave is pushing east of the region this morning \nwith rain tapering off. Sounding profiles still show very\nsaturated low level moisture with dry air moving aloft in the \nmid an upper levels. This will lead to widespread stratus \nlingering through the day and if we can generate some weak \nforcing we could see some areas of drizzles. Winds will \ngradually begin to turn to out of the northwest and the strong \nwinds that have been streaming over Lake Superior will also \ndecrease as surface high pressure builds across the Northern \nPlains. Wednesday, surface high pressure glides across the \nregion with mostly cloudy skies continuing. Highs on both days \nwill be in the 50s and 60s with overnight lows in the 30s. \n\nThursday and Friday:\n\nTwo low pressure systems are set to impact the Upper Midwest as we \nhead into the end of the work week. The first is being propelled out \nof the Central Rockies as a deep trough ejects out of the desert \nsouthwest. 00z suite of guidance largely has this system traversing \nacross the Midwest and towards Lake Michigan through Thursday. There \nstill remains some discrepancies on timing and placement but \nthe deep moisture from the Gulf looks to largely stay to the \nsoutheast of the Northland. While this low will largely bring \nrain chances across our southern counties another low advancing \nout of Saskatchewan will bring rain chances across our northern\ncounties. The latest NBM guidance suggests that rain will being\nThursday morning out of the south with chances spreading across\nthe region through Thursday night and tapering off Friday \nmorning. The trajectory of the northern low will bring an onset \nof cold air with it pouring in from the northwest. If the cold \nair manages to arrive while we still have moisture to work with \nwe could see some snow mixing in with the rain across northern \nMN. The GEFs (which has a slightly slow progression of the \nmoisture when compared to the EPS) has about 13% of its members \nindicating some snow activity. Overall, low impact with mostly \njust being conversational snow if it manages to develop. \nAdditionally, the area will see a strong pressure gradient \nsetting up leading to strong northwest winds. Depending on how \nquickly the precipitation exits we could see elevated fire \nweather concerns as RHs plummet. \n\nWeekend:\n\nThe aforementioned pair of lows will merge over the Great Lakes \nSaturday morning. The pressure gradient will relax a bit over the \nregion for Saturday but we will see quite a chilly morning to \nbegin the weekend. Wind chills will be in the teens and \ntwenties. Temperatures will recover through the day as plenty of\nsunshine is expected, look for highs to climb back into the 50s\nand 60s. Conditions will also be quite dry which may prompt \nsome fire weather headlines again. Sunday is shaping up to be \nbeautiful with highs in the 60s and lighter winds out of the \nwest and southwest.",
